<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    OTD Interop — headless integration with OpenTabletDriver (#807).

    Connections, session lifetime, and safe settings operations. No UI, no artist-facing policy, and
    NO reference to OpenTabletArtist: the app depends on this library, never the reverse. That direction
    is the whole point, and a reference the other way would silently undo it.

    net10.0 matches the only consumer. This is a library that splits one application, not one published
    for broad consumption, so it does not multi-target. A .NET 8 consumer could not reference it; revisit
    if a named consumer ever needs that, and only with dependency/API checks and tests on each runtime.
    (https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/net-standard)

    It bundles no runtime. OtdInterop.dll runs on its host application's runtime, which for OTA is the
    single .NET 10 that its self-contained publish already supplies. Nothing here changes the separate
    OpenTabletDriver daemon's own runtime prerequisite, which is a property of that process.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>OtdInterop</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>OtdInterop</RootNamespace>
    <LangVersion>14</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- The boundary is only worth having if it is enforced. XML docs are on because the contracts
         here (ownership, execution, session lifetime) live in doc comments and are the deliverable. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One reference. OpenTabletDriver.Desktop transitively brings OpenTabletDriver, .Native,
    .Configurations and StreamJsonRpc, which is everything this library needs for Settings, Profile
    and the daemon protocol. Deliberately NOT a copy of OTA's dependency list: no Avalonia, no
    SkiaSharp, no MVVM toolkit. If something here needs one of those, it does not belong here.

    The submodule targets net8.0; referencing it from net10.0 is the allowed direction, and OTA has
    done exactly this since before the split.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\external\OpenTabletDriver\OpenTabletDriver.Desktop\OpenTabletDriver.Desktop.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    These three MUST match OpenTabletArtist.csproj exactly, and must move together with it.

    Not a copy of OTA's dependency list - they are the minimum this library needs, and they are pinned
    for a reason. The submodule references StreamJsonRpc 2.22.23, which drags in MessagePack 2.5.192;
    that trips NU1903 (GHSA-hv8m-jj95-wg3x, fixed in 2.5.301). OTA already pins past it, so before these
    lines existed this project reintroduced 44 advisory warnings the app had already dealt with.

    Matching versions is also a correctness requirement, not just tidiness: both assemblies load into
    one process, so exactly one version of each wins at runtime. If these drift from OTA's, which one
    that is stops being obvious. Dependabot groups them by coupling for the same reason (#752/#757).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="StreamJsonRpc" Version="2.25.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="MessagePack" Version="2.5.303" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Test-only visibility for the internal writer and the internal connection.

    Both are internal because both can reach past the protected settings path: SettingsFileStore writes
    the file the daemon is running from, and DaemonClient writes settings to the daemon itself. Nothing
    outside should be able to construct either. They still need tests of their own - atomic replacement,
    the last-known-good fallback, an unwritable destination, the reconnect coalescing, the debug-stream
    reference count - and those are properties of the implementations rather than of the interfaces, so
    they cannot be written from outside.

    The production app is deliberately not granted access, and that is the load-bearing part: nothing
    shipping can reach the raw writer.

    The four grants below are the FINAL arrangement, not a way-station: #807 Phase 6 moved the library's
    tests into their own project, and what remains is deliberate and explained per entry. The two app test
    projects are here because they compile the library suite's fakes, which implement internal interfaces;
    relocating every such test would buy nothing that the app-not-granted rule does not already give.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The library's own suite, which is where its tests belong: it references OtdInterop and nothing
         else, so it can only exercise what the library actually offers. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OtdInterop.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenTabletArtist.UiTests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenTabletArtist.Tests" />
    <!-- The switch-check tool drives NoteConnectedDaemon directly: its unreadable-daemon scenario has to
         ask the session to look again without a real reconnect, since making a daemon unreadable means
         elevating it and that needs a UAC prompt. A diagnostic seam, not production surface. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OtdDaemonSwitchCheck" />
    <!-- The UI suite compiles the same FakeDaemonTransport source, which implements the internal
         settings channel; without this it sees a type the app is not meant to see, and cannot build. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenTabletArtist.UiTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
